<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mehdi Frashëri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mehdi Frashëri was an Albanian politician who served as prime minister in 1935 to 1936. After the German occupation of Albania in 1943 he headed the High Regency Council of the nominally independent Albanian state, seeking to protect the country while working with the Germans. He left Albania in 1944 and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mehdi-frasheri.jpg" alt="Mehdi Frashëri" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mehdi_Frash%C3%ABri_(portrait).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frashëri was born on 28 February 1872 in Frashër, in the Janina Vilayet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, the son of Ragip bey, kaymakam of Metsovo. He studied in Konica and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Monastir</a> and graduated from the Mekteb-i Mülkiye in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> in 1897. In 1901 he was charged in Istanbul with setting up, with a small group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>, a press in a cellar to print Albanian nationalist material.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He served as kaymakam of Peqin from 1901 to 1903 and then joined the Secret Committee for the Liberation of Albania in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> he was governor of the Mutasarrifate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. As a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-commission-of-control">International Commission of Control</a> he signed the Protocol of Corfu on 17 May 1914, and he was mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> under Prince Wied, a minister in 1918 and minister of the interior in 1920. He took part in the Congress of Durrës in December 1918, represented Albania at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> in 1923 and in the early 1930s sat on the committees reforming the civil code.</p><p>Frashëri opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>'s invasion of Albania, broadcast attacks on it and refused to sign a declaration in support of the Italians, taking refuge in the Turkish Legation. He was later arrested and interned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 1942 he was honorary chairman of the Conference of Pezë, which gathered Albanian anti-fascists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After the capitulation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> the Germans sought him out, and on 16 September he agreed to come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a> for talks with Hermann Neubacher, Major Franz von Scheiger and Martin Schliep. It was agreed that Albania would have its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> under Nazi Germany, on the model of the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, and Frashëri became regent and head of the council. Its chair was meant to rotate, but Lef Nosi declined for health reasons and Anton Harapi refused, as a Catholic monk, any post in which he would have to sanction the death penalty. When the Germans withdrew they took Frashëri with them; he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and then settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mehdi Frashëri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Seeking local support, Germany declared Albania independent, convened a national assembly and set up a Regency Council headed by <b>Mehdi Frashëri</b>, with Albanian governments led successively by figures such as Rexhep Mitrovica.</blockquote><a href="/state/albanian-kingdom-1943-1944">Albanian Kingdom (1943-1944)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Frashër, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.37,20.43&amp;year=1931" aria-label="Frashër on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5052 721 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mehdi Frashëri"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5502 991h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5502 991h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.37,20.43&amp;year=1931">What was here in 1931? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
